      "title": "Kalshi’s Tarek Mansour Wants to ‘Depolarize the Conversation’",
      "url": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/trump-teleprompter-operator-ordered-pay-172k-prediction-bets-kalshi-rcna594976",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T21:25:15.804Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/white-house/trump-teleprompter-operator-ordered-pay-172k-prediction-bets-kalshi-rcna594976",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "White House teleprompter operator ordered to pay $172K for placing prediction market bets on Trump’s speeches",
      "pageDescription": "The Commodity Futures Trading Commission announced the penalties for Gabriel Perez, who made the bets through Kalshi.",
      "evidenceText": "White House teleprompter operator ordered to pay $172K for placing prediction market bets on Trump’s speeches IE 11 is not supported. For an optimal experience visit our site on another browser. Skip to Content Gabriel Perez was a technical assistant to the president who had served as a teleprompter operator for Trump since 2016. John Moore / Getty Images file Share Add NBC News to Google Aug. 28, 2026, 9:59 PM EDT By Phil Helsel A teleprompter operator for President Donald Trump who made online prediction trades based on information from upcoming speeches has been ordered to pay $172,000, a federal agency said Friday night. Gabriel Perez used “material, nonpublic information” he obtained through his job to place “trades” on a prediction market, the Commodity Futures Trading Commission said in a news release . Perez was ordered to pay the money he made in profits, $107,539.02, as well as a civil penalty of $65,000. He’s also banned from trading on prediction markets for three years, the CFTC said. Perez made the trades through Kalshi. The CFTC said Kalshi assisted in the matter. “A Kalshi surveillance investigation caught a White House staffer engaging in prohibited trading activity. Today this individual was subjected to penalties by the CFTC and by our exchange,” Bobby DeNault, Kalshi’s lead lawyer, said in a post on X . “It doesn’t matter who you are: violate our rules or federal law and you will face the consequences,” he added. The White House did not immediately respond to an email seeking comment Friday night. Perez could not be reached for comment. WH teleprompter operator on leave for alleged insider trading on Trump's speeches 02:41 The CFTC called it a case of insider trading, adding that Perez traded “presidential mention market contracts, which are event contracts reflecting words or phrases the President may use during his speeches.” For Subscribers FOR SUBSCRIBERS Trump’s DOJ says its mission is ‘unchanged’ after GOP’s midterm losses FOR SUBSCRIBERS Ron DeSantis makes his first move toward a possible presidential run FOR SUBSCRIBERS The House districts that remain uncalled after the 2026 midterms FOR SUBSCRIBERS The CDC weighs new vaccine strategies as measles surges across the U.S. FOR SUBSCRIBERS MAHA rejoices as Stephen Miller takes over as chief of staff FOR SUBSCRIBERS Democrats have been waiting to put Elon Musk under oath. Their chance is coming. FOR SUBSCRIBERS How the Kremlin turned out the lights at Russia’s top independent newspaper FOR SUBSCRIBERS The Data Dive: Steve Kornacki analyzes Texas’ big blue shift FOR SUBSCRIBERS Federal chip smuggling probe shifted after Nvidia contacted investigators FOR SUBSCRIBERS Trump inadvertently led emissions to fall by going to war with Iran, research shows FOR SUBSCRIBERS How Congress’ post-midterms session is shaping up FOR SUBSCRIBERS Big tasks await as Congress returns after Democratic surge FOR SUBSCRIBERS They fled Democratic-run states to escape liberal policies. Then the blue wave hit. FOR SUBSCRIBERS Oregon man charged with taking Utah teen had been accused of sexually abusing 6-year-old FOR SUBSCRIBERS U.S. skips the COP31 climate summit as 1.5C global warming limit slips away Slide 1 of 1 For Subscribers FOR SUBSCRIBERS Trump’s DOJ says its mission is ‘unchanged’ after GOP’s midterm losses FOR SUBSCRIBERS Ron DeSantis makes his first move toward a possible presidential run FOR SUBSCRIBERS The House districts that remain uncalled after the 2026 midterms FOR SUBSCRIBERS The CDC weighs new vaccine strategies as measles surges across the U.S. FOR SUBSCRIBERS MAHA rejoices as Stephen Miller takes over as chief of staff FOR SUBSCRIBERS Democrats have been waiting to put Elon Musk under oath. Their chance is coming. FOR SUBSCRIBERS How the Kremlin turned out the lights at Russia’s top independent newspaper FOR SUBSCRIBERS The Data Dive: Steve Kornacki analyzes Texas’ big blue shift FOR SUBSCRIBERS Federal chip smuggling probe shifted after Nvidia contacted investigators FOR SUBSCRIBERS Trump inadvertently led emissions to fall by going to war with Iran, research shows FOR SUBSCRIBERS How Congress’ post-midterms session is shaping up FOR SUBSCRIBERS Big tasks await as Congress returns after Democratic surge FOR SUBSCRIBERS They fled Democratic-run states to escape liberal policies. Then the blue wave hit. FOR SUBSCRIBERS Oregon man charged with taking Utah teen had been accused of sexually abusing 6-year-old FOR SUBSCRIBERS U.S. skips the COP31 climate summit as 1.5C global warming limit slips away Slide 1 of 1 Perez was a technical assistant to the president who had served as a teleprompter operator for Trump since 2016. The CFTC cited what it called Perez’s “exemplary cooperation” in the case when announcing the fine and penalties Friday. Then-White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt in July called the online trades a breach of ethics and “deeply unfortunate and frankly a disgrace.” Perez was placed on leave in July and Leavitt said at the time that “this individual will no longer be here.” Share Add NBC News to Google Phil Helsel Phil Helsel is a reporter for NBC News. Steve Kopack contributed .",
