      "id": "d6793998ea0201dae13452505cdaa9fae0dec0bc12f3568107d335fb5c05aedb",
      "title": "Haley Stevens’ stunning endorsement set off a media firestorm. Will Michigan voters care?",
      "url": "https://mailchi.mp/poynter/haley-stevens-stunning-endorsement-set-off-a-media-firestorm-will-michigan-voters-care",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T21:25:15.804Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://mailchi.mp/poynter/haley-stevens-stunning-endorsement-set-off-a-media-firestorm-will-michigan-voters-care",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "The Michigan Democrat withdrew her support for Abdul El-Sayed and endorsed Republican Mike Rogers just days before Election Day",
      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt The Michigan Democrat withdrew her support for Abdul El-Sayed and endorsed Republican Mike Rogers just days before Election Day Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION Haley Stevens&rsquo; stunning endorsement set off a media firestorm. Will Michigan voters care? Rep. Haley Stevens, D-Mich., speaks alongside Michigan Republican Senate candidate Mike Rogers after a church service in Detroit on Sunday. (AP Photo/Jose Juarez) On the eve of the pivotal midterm elections, there was a bombshell development in one of the nation&rsquo;s most important and hotly contested races. Michigan Democratic Congresswoman Haley Stevens withdrew her support for Michigan Democratic Senate candidate Abdul El-Sayed and is publicly endorsing his Republican rival, Mike Rogers. El-Sayed defeated Stevens in the Democratic primary, but up until last week, Stevens had endorsed El-Sayed. Then came Sunday&rsquo;s stunning developments, which have sent shockwaves throughout the political and media world. Stevens put out a video on Sunday endorsing Rogers. In the video, she said, &ldquo;Hey Michigan, as you may have seen, our Democratic nominee for U.S. Senate, Abdul, said that I was no longer welcome in his campaign and that I should stay away. And he&rsquo;s right. And that&rsquo;s why I am endorsing Mike Rogers for the United States Senate today.&rdquo; Despite her endorsement of a Republican in a race that could very well decide the balance of power in the Senate, Stevens said, &ldquo;I&rsquo;m still a very proud Democrat.&rdquo; Well, maybe not. More on that in a second. The New York Times&rsquo; Bayliss Wagner, Reid J. Epstein and Tim Balk wrote , &ldquo;The extraordinary development reflected both the bitter disagreements over Israel and the personal animosity between the two prominent Michigan Democrats. Ms. Stevens, a moderate and a strong supporter of Israel, often clashed with Dr. El-Sayed, an outspoken progressive critic of the country, during their contentious primary race. After he narrowly beat her in August, she immediately endorsed him and they seemed to reach a quiet truce.&rdquo; The Washington Post&rsquo;s Violet Jira and Erin Cox wrote that &ldquo;the Michigan race has been mired in tensions since a bitter primary battle that ended with El-Sayed&rsquo;s narrow defeat of Stevens and that exposed deep rifts in the party over Israel. Those tensions escalated last week after Stevens demanded that El-Sayed denounce a video widely criticized as antisemitic from a group that is not affiliated with his campaign. Attempts to unify the party instead blew up.&rdquo; Jira and Cox added, &ldquo;While Stevens&rsquo;s clash with El-Sayed appears highly personal, it also highlights fault lines within the Democratic Party over Israel that are likely to persist in Congress after the November elections and into the 2028 campaign for the presidential nomination.&rdquo; Stevens&rsquo; social media endorsement came out shortly after she appeared at a Detroit church on Sunday morning with Rogers. Appearing at a different church in Detroit, El-Sayed said, &ldquo;If you are somebody who believes that we should stand up to hate, and you have watched this last campaign, I think we probably would not want to support Mike Rogers and Donald Trump and JD Vance. None of this matters.&rdquo; Apparently talking about Rogers and Stevens, El-Sayed added , &ldquo;This is a state of 10 million people who deserve a lot better from their government. I'm a lot more focused on the 9,999,998 that are not the ones you guys are talking about.&rdquo; Reaction from politicians and media was swift and relentless on Sunday. In a multipart tweet , Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer called Stevens&rsquo; endorsement of Rogers a &ldquo;despicable betrayal.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;We Democrats are equally united in our total revulsion at the racist, Islamophobic, and over-the-top false attacks on Abdul by Republicans.&rdquo; Stevens&rsquo; chief of staff, Justin German, resigned and said he was &ldquo;shocked and heartbroken&rdquo; by Stevens&rsquo; endorsement of Rogers. The Detroit Free Press&rsquo; Clara Hendrickson and Todd Spangler reported that more than half of Stevens&rsquo; staff has resigned. The state&rsquo;s other senator, Democrat Elissa Slotkin, has largely stayed out of the fighting between her fellow Democrats, but she weighed in Sunday. She said she has stayed out of it because her constituents are more concerned about the price of gas and groceries than political infighting. &ldquo;And it is for that reason,&rdquo; Slotkin wrote on X , &ldquo;that I have avoided commenting on the actions of Congresswoman Haley Stevens until now.&rdquo; She added, &ldquo;But there is simply too much at stake. We have to be focused on the mission at hand. That mission is to put a check on President Trump in the U.S. House and Senate, and do the hard work of saving the middle class. So anyone who makes an election this important about themselves, and not Michiganders and our democracy, should think hard about whether they are serving the greater good.&rdquo; Meanwhile, Michigan Democratic Party chair Curtis Hertel tweeted , &ldquo;Congresswoman Stevens is not a current member of the Michigan Democratic Party, nor will she be in the future.&rdquo; Of course, Republicans are cheering Stevens&rsquo; decision. In a Truth Social post , President Donald Trump wrote, &ldquo;wow, this is BIG.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;She showed great and almost unprecedented loyalty to the Great People of Michigan with what she did. It&rsquo;s a thing called bravery, and few people have it like just displayed by Congresswoman Haley Stevens!&rdquo; At a rally in Iowa, Trump said he &ldquo;would love&rdquo; for Stevens to join the Republican Party. House Speaker Mike Johnson said, &ldquo;She&rsquo;s standing for truth. Haley&rsquo;s a good person.&rdquo; And Rogers himself said, &ldquo;This is courage in politics in America today. It takes courage for someone to stand up and say we can&rsquo;t do that.&rdquo; Among media reactions was Jon Favreau, former Barack Obama speechwriter and co-host of the \"Pod Save America\" podcast, who tweeted , &ldquo;This is the single most unproductive, selfish thing a Democratic candidate has done all cycle and likely many cycles before that. Having misjudged candidates in the past myself, I hope everyone who tried to recruit, support, and defend Haley can now admit what a mistake it was.&rdquo; Zeteo&rsquo;s Mehdi Hasan tweeted , &ldquo;WTF?&rdquo; That was one of a dozen tweets Hasan posted about Stevens on Sunday. He also wrote , &ldquo;She&rsquo;s clearly a disturbed, deranged, dishonest woman (less than 48 hours ago she was swearing to the NYT she was backing Abdul) but the bigger question to ask is: how on earth did the Dem establishment put her forward as their candidate? A complete traitor to their party and now an enabler of Trump? There needs to be a lot of soul-searching among centrist former Stevens supporters. Schumer needs to say something for a start!&rdquo; Appearing on MS NOW, Hasan blasted Stevens. He pointed out that one of Stevens&rsquo; issues is that she claims El-Sayed won&rsquo;t condemn bigotry. &ldquo;He won&rsquo;t pull down an ad he had nothing to do with,&rdquo; Hasan said. &ldquo;The irony is she is with Mike Rogers &mdash; a man who has pushed some of the most disgusting racism. &hellip; This is a man who went on social media and when somebody said Abdul El-Sayed is a terrorist, he said he absolutely agrees with them. He has suggested Abdul El-Sayed is a part of the Muslim Brotherhood and suggested he agreed with 9/11. He has pushed ridiculous racism against this Muslim Senate candidate.&rdquo; Journalist and political commentator Molly Jong-Fast suggested it would be the end of Stevens&rsquo; political career. Ultimately, the question is: Does Stevens&rsquo; about-face endorsement of Rogers even matter? Will this even hurt El-Sayed? Hard to say, but according to many reports, it does spotlight a fracture inside the Democratic Party. Dan Pfeiffer, co-host of &ldquo;Pod Save America,&rdquo; wrote , &ldquo;Her endorsement of Rogers certainly doesn&rsquo;t help. You want a united party down the stretch and the ability to deliver your closing message without distraction. Haley Stevens&rsquo; megalomaniacal narcissism makes that impossible. However, I don't think it's a game changer. Stevens has no loyal base of support waiting to hear what she has to say. Her campaign had zero grassroots support. She couldn't build crowds, raise money, or get tons of volunteers. Super PACs, AIPAC, and concerns about Abdul propped her up. Her endorsement has little sway.&rdquo; He added this for context: &ldquo;Two million people have already voted in Michigan. Based on the turnout in 2018 and 2020, that should be about 45% of the overall electorate. It's worth asking how many swing voters will consume or care about this drama down the stretch.&rdquo; For what it&rsquo;s worth, the latest New York Times/Siena poll , published Sunday evening, has El-Sayed leading Rogers by 11 points. What happened? Last week, Stevens&rsquo; fianc&eacute; announced he would vote for Rogers. Semafor reported that Stevens&rsquo; staffers were concerned that she might do the same, so they asked her about it. She reportedly told them via text message, &ldquo;Omg why would you think I would do that?!!&rdquo; But that set up Sunday&rsquo;s explosive developments. The Washington Post&rsquo;s Dan Merica, Karen Tumulty, Liz Goodwin and Erin Cox reported that Stevens told a longtime friend Saturday evening that she planned to attend a church service with El-Sayed. Instead, she went to the church that Rogers was attending, with the Post reporting, &ldquo;Not only did she hug Rogers on the way in and sit beside him in the pews, but shortly after, she announced a surprise endorsement for the Republican, calling him the best choice for a seat that could decide the Senate&rsquo;s balance of power.&rdquo; How shocking was all this? Apparently, even Rogers&rsquo; camp didn&rsquo;t know it was coming. Check out the Post story for more details on how this thing blew up. And here&rsquo;s interesting work from Semafor&rsquo;s Nicholas Wu and David Weigel: &ldquo;&lsquo;Dumbfounded&rsquo;: Democrats&rsquo; chaos in Michigan exposes party&rsquo;s divides.&rdquo; &lsquo;60 Minutes&rsquo; feature In a bit of good and bad timing, &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; ran a segment on El-Sayed and Rogers Sunday night. Correspondent Ross Douthat interviewed both candidates, although the feature was clearly put together before the big news of Stevens&rsquo; endorsement. The program did mention the endorsement at the top of the show, but it was too late to ask either candidate about it. Disturbing comments President Donald Trump dances as he departs after speaking during a campaign rally in Iowa on Sunday. (AP Photo/Charlie Neibergall) Appearing at a rally over the weekend, Trump talked about Abdul El-Sayed and, as he often does, called him &ldquo;Mohammed.&rdquo; During his remarks, Trump said he calls El-Sayed that because &ldquo;I'm the president, and I have the right to call you whatever the hell I want.&rdquo; That wasn&rsquo;t the only ridiculous thing Trump said at a rally. He also said that The New York Times should be &ldquo;prosecuted&rdquo; for putting out what he called &ldquo;fake polls&rdquo; that look bad for Republicans. Some good AI journalism Here&rsquo;s a good use of artificial intelligence to assist in reporting. The New York Times&rsquo; Peter Baker and Dylan Freedman published a piece Sunday: &ldquo;Threats, Gaffes and Men&rsquo;s Posteriors: Trump&rsquo;s Talk Gets Wilder.&rdquo; Baker is the Times&rsquo; chief White House correspondent and Freedman is the Times&rsquo; AI projects editor. Following up on their 2024 reporting, they reviewed Trump&rsquo;s public speeches and found he has been &ldquo;more profane, more absolutist, longer-winded and less coherent than he was in 2016.&rdquo; Baker and Freedman wrote, &ldquo;He can be forgetful and repetitive. He is more long-winded, more profane and more absolutist than when he ran for president a decade ago. His rambling speeches seem even more garbled at times. He makes comments that leave people wondering what he is talking about or that sound like he is saying something he is not. And even for a president who has never fully observed the norms of the office, he seems more willing to say things no other president would say.&rdquo; So how did the Times determine this? At the bottom of the story, they described their methodology: The Times collected thousands of Mr. Trump&rsquo;s speeches, interviews, social media posts and schedules using data from Roll Call&rsquo;s Factbase from 2015 through late October this year. To quantify Mr. Trump&rsquo;s use of all-or-nothing language and swearwords, we measured basic word frequency statistics across Mr. Trump&rsquo;s presidential terms and campaign periods. To identify confusing and rambling passages of text, we used the A.I. model Claude Opus 5.5 to score more than 6,500 passages of Mr. Trump&rsquo;s speeches with detailed instructions based on our previous reporting analyzing Mr. Trump&rsquo;s use of rambling and profane language. We counted a speech passage as incoherent if it scored at least a seven out of 10 with this rubric, which was informed by manual review of hundreds of passages and an evaluation to ensure a high bar of accuracy. The Times then detailed what the results showed. Baker and Freedman wrote, &ldquo;The Times review from 2015 through Thursday found that the president in his second term has used 19 percent more all-or-nothing words like &lsquo;always,&rsquo; &lsquo;never,&rsquo; &lsquo;everything; and &lsquo;totally; at rallies than he did a decade ago and 5 percent more than in 2024, a sign of black-and-white thinking that experts say often accompanies advancing age or cognitive decline. He is cruder than he used to be, using nearly 15 swear words per 10,000 words compared with 14 two years ago and six a decade ago, a change consistent with age-related disinhibition. The review, relying on an A.I.-assisted metric, found that he makes rambling or confusing digressions in his rallies and speeches two and a half times as often in his second term as in the 2016 campaign, although roughly the same as in 2024. His vocabulary remains about the same, as does the reading level of his speaking, which hovers between fourth and fifth grade. (The average modern president speaks at an eighth-grade level.)&rdquo; They also found this interesting: &ldquo;Mr. Trump&rsquo;s public schedule is thinner, down to 1.3 events per weekday versus 2.1 in the first term. But he is posting online an average of 21 times a day, up from 13 a day in 2016 although down from 34 a day two years ago. The night owl president has posted messages between midnight and 5 a.m. on 30 percent of his nights in his second term and has had 240 bursts of 10 or more posts an hour.&rdquo; Trump loses another media case For the second time, a judge has tossed Trump&rsquo;s defamation lawsuit against the publisher of The Wall Street Journal for the publication&rsquo;s 2025 reporting on a bawdy birthday card sent to Jeffrey Epstein. This goes back to a Journal story published in July 2025 that reported a letter bearing Trump&rsquo;s name was sent to Epstein for his 50th birthday in 2003. Trump sued the paper, its parent company News Corp., its CEO, the reporters who wrote the story and News Corp.'s chairman emeritus Rupert Murdoch. He called it &ldquo;fake news&rdquo; and asked for $10 billion. U.S. District Judge Darrin P. Gayles threw out the suit in April, but did so without prejudice. That meant Trump could refile. In a 20-page ruling over the weekend, Gayles said Trump did not prove the Journal published the story knowing it was false or with reckless disregard for the truth. He added that the suit was &ldquo;grasping at straws.&rdquo; A spokesperson for the administration said Trump would appeal the decision, adding, &ldquo;President Trump will continue to press forward in his powerhouse lawsuit against the Wall Street Journal and all of the other defendants. The President will continue to hold those who mislead the American People with Fake News and smears accountable for their actions.&rdquo; A Dow Jones spokesperson said, &ldquo;We&rsquo;re pleased with the court&rsquo;s decision.&rdquo; Saturday Night Lives I&rsquo;ve always been a big fan of &ldquo;Saturday Night Live&rdquo; and have defended it from critics who say it&rsquo;s no longer funny. However, this season has gotten off to a rough start. That is, until this past Saturday&rsquo;s fourth episode of the season showed some signs of life. Host Inde Navarrette, the star of the surprise horror hit &ldquo;Obsession,&rdquo; was a breath of fresh air. It also never hurts to have a surprise appearance from Tom Hanks doing his classic David S. Pumpkins bit. But these days, if you&rsquo;re looking for good &ldquo;Saturday Night Live,&rdquo; you should go across the pond to &ldquo;Saturday Night Live U.K.&rdquo; The British version premiered in March and has run for 14 episodes so far. You can see all of them on Peacock. New York Times TV critic Caroline Darya Framke has a good breakdown in &ldquo;&lsquo;Saturday Night Live U.K.&rsquo; Has Found Its Voice.&rdquo; Media tidbits More on the Michigan Senate drama. The Atlantic&rsquo;s Jonathan Chait with &ldquo;Haley Stevens Is Sending the Wrong Message.&rdquo; For another perspective, here&rsquo;s The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s editorial board with &ldquo;Haley Stevens Breaks With Abdul El-Sayed Over Campaign &lsquo;Hate.&rsquo;&rdquo; Deadline&rsquo;s Glenn Garner with &ldquo;Savannah Guthrie Renews Plea For Information About Mom Nancy&rsquo;s Disappearance: &lsquo;We Cannot Move Forward.&rsquo;&rdquo; The Boston Globe&rsquo;s Aidan Ryan with &ldquo;Steve Kornacki&rsquo;s love of politics began at a Groton middle school.&rdquo; Mediaite&rsquo;s Joe DePaolo with &ldquo;Fox&rsquo;s Lara Trump Showcases &lsquo;Bipartisan&rsquo; Panel of 5 &lsquo;Michigan Voters&rsquo; &mdash; 4 of Whom Have Deep Ties to the Campaign of Republican Mike Rogers.&rdquo; The Athletic&rsquo;s Andrew Marchand digs into the &ldquo;Monday Night Football&rdquo; drama in &ldquo;&lsquo;I never thought you&rsquo;d do it to me&rsquo;: Inside Troy Aikman and Joe Buck&rsquo;s ESPN unraveling.&rdquo; ESPN and Buck/Aikman, by the way, have this season&rsquo;s Super Bowl. Marchand said on John Ourand&rsquo;s &ldquo;The Varsity&rdquo; podcast that there's a 5% chance Aikman won&rsquo;t do the Super Bowl because of this mess. Marchand&rsquo;s point is that it&rsquo;s a non-zero chance. Marchand said, &ldquo;Look, they&rsquo;re professional, but it&rsquo;s ugly. We&rsquo;re not helping (by) talking about it. We&rsquo;re not helping (by) writing about it. &hellip; These things happen and then maybe it shifts internally, you know, they read an article in The Athletic and they say, &lsquo;Wait, why aren&rsquo;t we just bringing these guys in a room and figuring it out.&rsquo; &hellip; The other 5% from him not doing it is that people are like, &lsquo;Wait, what the hell. Who&rsquo;s talking? What&rsquo;s going on? I&rsquo;m mad now and da da da,&rsquo; and it causes it to go",
