      "id": "da7fbb7ada195074f1621160d0b01ecb547b58c9b1cf52bd54a85fc8a162a548",
      "title": "Misread Mandate",
      "url": "https://www.cjr.org/analysis/how-we-watched-the-midterms-kornacki-piker-trump-tirade-burns-kalshi.php",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T21:25:15.804Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.cjr.org/analysis/how-we-watched-the-midterms-kornacki-piker-trump-tirade-burns-kalshi.php",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "How We Watched the Midterms - Columbia Journalism Review",
      "pageDescription": "Steve Kornacki and Hasan Piker went head-to-head, and Donald Trump called in.",
      "evidenceText": "How We Watched the Midterms - Columbia Journalism Review b AP Photo/Julia Demaree Nikhinson b Sign up for the daily CJR newsletter . On Tuesday night, millions of Americans tuned in to both television news and live Web streams as the midterm votes were tallied. Among those viewers was Donald Trump. At 11:16pm ET, around the time that networks started calling a Senate majority for Democrats, Dasha Burns, a coauthor of Politico ’s Playbook , called Trump in the middle of their livestream. “I wasn’t on the ballot and we didn’t get the people out. If I was on the ballot, we would’ve won it all very easily,” he told her. “We decided to make that call during the show, and we didn’t know whether he’d pick up,” Burns told me. “The questions I had for him were ones I’d been thinking about all fall, and I pressed him in real time to get as much detail as I could for our audience on how he saw the results and what it means for his presidency going forward.” Soon after, Trump called in to Fox News to unleash a twenty-three-minute tirade in which he deflected responsibility for the Republican Party’s unfolding catastrophe. On average, twenty-seven million people followed the results of Tuesday’s midterm elections live across the six biggest networks—five million more than in 2022, but far short of the forty-two million who tuned in during the presidential election two years ago. Independent and legacy-brand livestreams held their own, bolstering the case for a more personal, unfiltered, sometimes unapologetically partisan approach to political coverage. Meanwhile, the surging popularity of prediction market platforms as a venue for monitoring returns suggests that millions of people are finding new, more interactive ways to follow election results. NBC’s Steve Kornacki, the star data analyst, won the night, averaging more than 6.7 million viewers during prime time, according to Nielsen. Along with Kornacki Cam , a dedicated livestream, NBC’s election night coverage netted twenty-nine million views across all platforms . Kornacki originally planned to broadcast for thirteen consecutive hours but had to pull the plug at hour ten when “things caught up” to him, as he put it to fans. ABC came in second with 5.2 million viewers. Fox News—which Trump berated for its polls showing low presidential approval and for failing to sufficiently support him—came in third, with 4.32 million viewers, followed closely by CNN with 4.15 million. MS Now was fifth, with 3.75 million, but Ali Velshi’s VelshiCast, the network’s answer to Kornacki Cam, racked up nearly five and a half million views on YouTube. CBS came in last of the major networks, with 2.8 million viewers, along with similar numbers from YouTube. “We owned the night. We met our growing audiences where they are,” Bari Weiss, the editor in chief of CBS News, wrote in a memo to her team.  Online, independent creators and newer outlets came on strong. Hasan Piker, the left-wing political commentator and bête noire of Republicans and centrist Democrats, drew two hundred thousand concurrent viewers (about a hundred thousand fewer than Kornacki Cam had at the same time online) on his Twitch account. In contrast to the elaborate sets and high-tech touch screens on cable, Piker opted for a stripped-down aesthetic: a live feed from his home studio as he celebrated the Democratic landslide, mocked the Republicans-in-meltdown, and interviewed victorious Democratic congressional candidates he’d endorsed, including Colorado’s Melat Kiros and Pennsylvania’s Chris Rabb, all while shitposting on X. Brian Tyler Cohen, a progressive journalist, ran an even simpler show: a single-camera, ten-hour webcast in which he flipped among electoral maps, an austere setup that drew more than three million views.  Mehdi Hasan, the editor in chief of Zeteo , and its reporter Prem Thakker amassed more than a million total views on YouTube. The Bulwark , whose guests included Pete Buttigieg , had a similarly sized audience. Piers Morgan, the polarizing British media commentator, had more than seven hundred thousand viewers on his stream. On Rumble, the MAGA-preferred Twitch alternative, right-wing commentator Steven Crowder had over 2.2 million views and white supremacist Nick Fuentes had more than one million viewers over the course of the night. Mainstream outlets that have invested in video, including Politico and The Atlantic , ran shorter livestreams, although neither broke a hundred thousand views on YouTube—even if Politico created a viral moment when Burns called Trump . Other publications, such as the New York Times , the Washington Post , and the Wall Street Journal , stayed loyal to the electoral map and live-blog model, although the Times did also bring back its infamous “Needle,” the live tracker it had once retired . A spokesperson for the Journal told CJR that subscriber traffic jumped nearly 20 percent above the typical baseline on Tuesday. The Times declined to comment. The Post did not respond to a request for comment about Election Day traffic. Kalshi and Polymarket, the prediction market platforms, ran elaborate data visualizations based on their users’ betting odds. In effect, these platforms offered a contrast with online news outlets, conflating their odds with reporting and forecasting. Luana Lopes Lara, the Kalshi cofounder and COO, compared the Times ’ Needle with her company’s product, claiming that Kalshi forecast the blue tsunami faster and that “the future of news is real-time and markets-based.” Kalshi did not respond to a request for comment about its traffic, but trading volumes on its platform grew by hundreds of millions of dollars on Tuesday alone. “You don’t need a title, a large following, or an editor to publish you. All you need is a perspective and the conviction to back it,” Tarek Mansour, Kalshi’s CEO, wrote on X. In response, Nate Silver, the election data guru, pointed out something Mansour himself highlighted in our recent conversation : prediction market odds rely on data provided by polls and expert forecasters. “Give me a break. The polls and models provided the direction, in contrast to vibes and dumb insiders,” Silver said . “And yes, it’s good that traders were smart enough to trust them. But the better election models were more confident about the outcome than the markets and got there more quickly.” Editor’s Note : This piece has been corrected to reflect that Politico ’s call with the president occurred before his call with Fox News. Dasha Burns’s title has also been corrected. Has America ever needed a media defender more than now? Help us by joining CJR today . Ivan L. Nagy is a CJR Fellow. More from CJR Reporting the Backlash The midterms pushed data centers center stage, as media, activists, and politicians questioned AI’s ever-expanding infrastructure. By Lindsay Muscato   Novaya Gazeta ’s Journalism Will Live On The paper is shutting down. But a new project grants it electronic immortality. By Joel Simon   The Susan Collins Effect “It’s an image that’s cultivated by her PR people and by the Maine media to give the impression that she’s something she’s not.” By Rob Wolfe   The voice of journalism, since 1961 About Mission Masthead Privacy Policy Contact Support CJR Become a Member Donate Advertise Contact Us Copyright 2026, Columbia Journalism Review",
