      "id": "da8997ac7998698997b5a7f51b6aee924ba34d982dbe16bf654db35bac277b58",
      "title": "Meet our GIJC27 Co-Host, Conference Tickets, Guide to Size and Number Comparisons",
      "url": "https://www.journalismai.info/programmes/strategylab",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T21:25:15.804Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.journalismai.info/programmes/strategylab",
      "contentType": "text/html;charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "JournalismAI Strategy Lab &mdash; JournalismAI",
      "pageDescription": "The JournalismAI Strategy Lab is a FREE, regional online programme for journalists and media professionals who want to move beyond AI experimentation and make informed, strategic decisions about how AI fits into their journalism and their newsroom.",
      "evidenceText": "JournalismAI Strategy Lab &mdash; JournalismAI 0 JournalismAI S trategy Lab Africa NO-COST | 5 Weeks | Virtual |‍ ‍Apply by 29 November at 11:59 PM GMT AI with purpose and editorial clarity The JournalismAI Strategy Lab, previously the JournalismAI Academy , is a no-cost , five-week online programme for journalists and media professionals across Africa who want to move beyond experimenting with AI tools and start making informed, deliberate decisions about how AI fits into their journalism and their newsroom. The programme is designed by the JournalismAI team at the London School of Economics and Political Science (LSE) and supported by Google . When the Academy launched in 2021, the priority was helping newsrooms understand what AI was and what it could do. That work mattered. But the landscape has changed. AI tools are now part of journalists' daily work across the industry, and the questions have shifted: not whether to use AI, but how to use it well, with clarity, purpose, and editorial judgment. The programme has evolved to reflect that shift. The Strategy Lab is built to address today's most pressing questions. APPLICATIONS ARE OPEN! Now open to journalists across Africa. Previous editions of the programme focused on Sub-Saharan Africa. This year, for the first time, we are opening the programme to journalists and media professionals from across the entire continent, from North Africa to Southern Africa, and everywhere in between. We look forward to bringing together a cohort that reflects the diverse realities, challenges, and opportunities facing journalism in Africa. Each edition brings together a cohort of up to 20 participants from across Africa, creating a focused space for peer learning, shared challenges, and practical work. The programme combines expert-led masterclasses with hands-on workshops and structured peer discussion, all grounded in the realities that participants bring from their own newsrooms. African newsrooms face questions about AI that are often missing from global conversations, shaped by the languages, resources, and realities of the continent. The Strategy Lab creates space to tackle these questions together, drawing on the experience of peers working in similar conditions across the continent. The Strategy Lab does not assume technical knowledge. It does not require coding experience. It asks participants to come with curiosity, an openness to challenging their assumptions, and a commitment to take what they learn back to their organisations. Each edition is shaped by the context of the region it serves. Info Sessions We are also holding an information session to provide more details about the programme and any questions apart from those in the FAQs . Sign up to one of the below scheduled slots: Tuesday, 24 November 12:00 GMT / 13:00 WAT / 14:00 CAT 15:00 EAT How The Programme Works The Lab runs for over five weeks. The programme includes two sessions per week. Participants are expected to dedicate 5 hours per week to the programme, across the 5 weeks: Live presentations on Tuesday (2 hours) Live workshops on Thursday (2 hours) Assignment to complete individually before the following meeting (~60 minutes) The programme also includes an introductory week and a closing session, which take place on separate days across the five weeks. The Five Modules Each module is designed for participants who may be new to AI, or who have started experimenting but want to think more clearly about what they are doing and why. The learning is cumulative; each week adds a layer, and by the end, participants are equipped to view their own newsroom with fresh eyes and a clearer sense of next steps. Module 1 ➙ AI in Practice: Concepts & Applications [26 & 28 January] The starting point is not tools; it is understanding. This module covers what machine learning, large language models, and neural networks actually are; how they produce their outputs; and where their limitations lie. We look at how AI is currently used in journalism: what has worked, what has not, and the questions newsrooms are still working through. The aim is a grounded, honest picture of the landscape. Module instructor: TBC Module 2 ➙ Data, Tools & Workflow [2 & 4 February] AI is only as useful as the workflow it sits inside. This module covers how to evaluate a tool before adoption, the data AI applications rely on, and the practical questions of integrating AI into editorial workflows. We cover the difference between general-purpose tools and purpose-built newsroom solutions, and how to assess which approach suits a given organisation. Module instructor: TBC Module 3 ➙ GenerativeAI for Journalism [9 & 11 February] GenerativeAI is already part of many newsroom workflows. This module looks at strategic opportunities for innovation in journalism across the news value chain and where overreliance creates risk. Participants work directly with the tools, critically evaluating outputs and knowing when generative AI belongs in a workflow and when it does not. Module instructor: TBC Module 4 ➙ Risks, Ethics & Editorial Governance [16 & 17 February] Using AI without clear governance is both a reputational and an editorial risk. This module covers the main risks AI adoption brings to newsrooms: bias in tools and datasets, AI-generated disinformation and synthetic media, audience trust, and the consequences of moving without internal alignment. We look at what responsible AI use looks like in practice and how newsrooms are approaching editorial policies. Module instructor: TBC Module 5 ➙ From Ideation to Implementation [23 February & 25 February] This module is all about turning ideas into action. Participants work through a structured process to develop concrete proposals for AI applications in their newsroom, projects, workflows, or policies. They also have the chance to create simple prototypes or demonstrations of their concepts, giving a tangible sense of how their ideas could work in practice. By the end, participants leave with practical next steps they can start immediately and a cohort of peers to continue learning with beyond the programme. Module instructor: TBC What You Will Get Participants leave the Strategy Lab with more than a certificate. By the end of the five weeks, you will have: A grounded understanding of AI in journalism - what it is, what it can do, and where its limits lie Hands-on experience evaluating and working with AI tools in a structured environment A concrete action plan or project proposal to take back to your newsroom A network of peers from your region and beyond, working through the same challenges [ { \"shape\": \"underlineCurve\", \"isFront\": false, \"isAnimationEnabled\": false, \"animation\": \"draw\", \"duration\": 0.5, \"direction\": \"right\", \"color\": { \"type\": \"THEME_COLOR\" }, \"thickness\": { \"unit\": \"em\", \"value\": 0.1 }, \"linecap\": \"square\",",
