      "id": "e18c4c16aad35b0b07eea34b104d3f7b61f65e250c53060b20c6d302afe1f92a",
      "title": "Today is the big day … for the pollsters",
      "url": "https://mailchi.mp/poynter/today-is-the-big-day-for-the-pollsters",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T21:25:15.804Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://mailchi.mp/poynter/today-is-the-big-day-for-the-pollsters",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "Polls point to a strong night for Democrats, but Election Day will test whether pollsters have learned from big Trump-era misses",
      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt Polls point to a strong night for Democrats, but Election Day will test whether pollsters have learned from big Trump-era misses Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION Today is the big day &hellip; for the pollsters A person drops a vote-by-mail ballot on Oct. 27 during early voting in the 2026 midterm elections in Doral, Fla. (AP Photo/Lynne Sladky) Today, the American people will head to the ballot box and cast their votes in a hotly contested and pivotal midterm election. By this time tomorrow, we might finally get an answer to a question that the entire country has been waiting for. I&rsquo;m not talking about whether the nation is red or blue, conservative or liberal, right or left. I&rsquo;m talking about whether we can finally trust the polls. As we head into Election Day, most polls seem to suggest that it&rsquo;s going to be an excellent night for Democrats &mdash; a strong &ldquo;blue wave&rdquo; if not perhaps even a &ldquo;blue tsunami.&rdquo; But we&rsquo;ve been down this road before, never more starkly than in 2016, when polls suggested Hillary Clinton would defeat Donald Trump to become president. Since then, trust in polls has diminished, mostly because they have been consistently inconsistent. And why? The Trump Effect. Pollsters have had difficulty pinning down just how voters feel about Trump. Do Trump supporters admit to pollsters that they are Trump supporters? Do Trump supporters avoid talking to pollsters and, thus, skew the polls? As CNN&rsquo;s Brian Stelter noted , &ldquo;Democrats are a lot more eager to answer pollsters&rsquo; questions right now. Republicans are generally harder to reach.&rdquo; And, well, at the end of the day, polls are just polls. Polls are not elections. So can we trust them? Nate Cohn, chief political analyst for The New York Times, told his colleague Patrick Healy , &ldquo;You can trust them to a degree. You can trust the polls to usually be in the ballpark, but you can&rsquo;t trust them to be exactly &lsquo;right.&rsquo; Polls are inherently imprecise and subject to many sources of error. So if by &lsquo;trust&rsquo; you mean &lsquo;assume that the leading candidate will win,&rsquo; then the answer is &lsquo;no.&rsquo; The polls have been wrong by this standard before and they will be wrong again. But if trust means &lsquo;rely on the polls as the best source of information on the electorate, even if an inexact one,&rsquo; then the answer is yes.&rdquo; Cohn&rsquo;s answer is reasonable and fair, but that doesn&rsquo;t make it any less frustrating. The reality is that we as voters read the polls and then become confused and even embittered when the results don&rsquo;t perfectly align with them. William P. Davis, director of election data analytics for The New York Times, told Healy, &ldquo;Polls measure public opinion at a specific point in time. If people are still making up their minds, or late-breaking information upends a race, any prior poll will miss it. Polls can help us understand which races are close, and why; we won&rsquo;t know until the results are in how they will shake out.&rdquo; Pollsters have tried to account for &mdash; and correct &mdash; the Trump Effect. Cohn said, &ldquo;The thing that we&rsquo;ve learned over the last decade &mdash; and that the industry as a whole has learned &mdash; is that even a perfectly designed survey will probably underrepresent Trump&rsquo;s supporters. &hellip; We&rsquo;ve adopted a new method for &lsquo;weighting,&rsquo; the technique pollsters use to give more weight to underrepresented groups. The new approach lets us leverage the rich data we have on our survey respondents while weighting the poll to more thoroughly represent the population. This approach improved our past polls when we took the data from our old surveys and applied the new weighting approach. It would have especially helped in the white working-class states where polls have erred in the past, but that&rsquo;s no guarantee of better results in this cycle or the future.&rdquo; In other words, it is still not a precise science. And polls do warn us, and always have, that there are margins of error. So, yes, polling suggests that tonight is going to be a good night for Democrats, but we won&rsquo;t know until we actually see the results. Having said that, Cohn wrote in his &ldquo;The Tilt&rdquo; newsletter that midterm polls in the Trump era have had a good track record. Cohn wrote, &ldquo;There&rsquo;s a lot of evidence suggesting that Mr. Trump&rsquo;s supporters aren&rsquo;t just less likely to respond to surveys, but also less likely to vote in midterm elections. This would help explain the basic pattern of polling error during the Trump era: Polls have a pretty good record in midterms but not in presidential elections.&rdquo; And, oh, as far as talk of a &ldquo;blue tsunami?&rdquo; That&rsquo;s what the usually respected New York Times/Siena University Poll is suggesting. Famed pollster Nate Silver wrote in his &ldquo;Silver Bulletin&rdquo; newsletter , &ldquo;They might be wrong again, but it&rsquo;s getting hard to deny that polls point toward a really big year for Democrats.&rdquo; Tonight&rsquo;s coverage The Decision Desk for NewsNation&rsquo;s election night coverage. (Courtesy of NewsNation) All the big anchors will be on the air tonight, bringing you election coverage. That means David Muir (ABC), Savannah Guthrie, Tom Llamas, Kristen Welker, and Hallie Jackson (NBC); Tony Dokoupil and Margaret Brennan (CBS); Rachel Maddow (MS NOW); Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum (Fox News); and Jake Tapper and Anderson Cooper (CNN). Of course, those hosts will be joined by dozens of reporters, analysts and commentators &mdash; such as big board election experts including CNN&rsquo;s John King and NBC&rsquo;s Steve Kornacki. Status&rsquo; Natalie Korach notes , &ldquo;Nearly every major television network will be drawing from the same source of data, provided by The Associated Press, the nonprofit organization that has been tallying votes across the country since 1848. It will mark the first time since 2016 that all the major broadcast networks and most cable news networks will rely on the AP's election data, after many turned to Edison Research in 2024.&rdquo; Korach added, &ldquo;And the organization's reach extends well beyond television. Major print publications, including The New York Times, The Washington Post, and The Wall Street Journal, will also rely on AP vote counts, while partnerships with Google and OpenAI will put its results in front of an even larger swath of the American public.&rdquo; Meanwhile, NewsNation is trying something it compares to the National Football League&rsquo;s &ldquo;RedZone.&rdquo; &ldquo;RedZone&rdquo; quickly jumps among NFL games on Sundays to show viewers the most consequential action as it happens. For election night, the network will take viewers inside the process of calling races. NewsNation will partner with election data provider Decision Desk HQ for independent race call information. The coverage will be co-anchored by Chris Cuomo, Elizabeth Vargas, Leland Vittert, Katie Pavlich and Chris Stirewalt. Also, here&rsquo;s Forbes contributor Mark Joyella with &ldquo;Julio Vaqueiro And Noticias Telemundo Will Cover Midterms With Emphasis On Latino Voters.&rdquo; A disaster of an interview As we head into Election Day, there is still plenty of buzz about the weekend&rsquo;s events when Democratic Rep. Haley Stevens of Michigan flipped her Senate endorsement from fellow Democrat Abdul El-Sayed to Republican Mike Rogers. I wrote about this extensively in Monday&rsquo;s newsletter . What I didn&rsquo;t include was Stevens&rsquo; CNN appearance Sunday night and an interview with Anderson Cooper that was nothing short of bizarre. Her attempt to explain her decision to back Rogers was even more confusing than her actual decision to back Rogers. Variety&rsquo;s Alison Herman wrote , &ldquo;Watching Congresswoman Haley Stevens attempt to explain her 11th-hour endorsement of Republican Mike Rogers over her fellow Democrat Abdul El-Sayed in Tuesday&rsquo;s election to become the next United States Senator from Michigan, my primary feeling was one of relief. Thank God this person didn&rsquo;t beat El-Sayed in the primary, despite outspending him in a landslide. Thank God this person isn&rsquo;t eligible for higher office, now putting her unfitness to serve on full display. Thank God this person won&rsquo;t be on our television screens much longer.&rdquo; Herman called Stevens&rsquo; interview &ldquo;excruciating.&rdquo; Kudos to Cooper for pressing Stevens on why she switched her endorsement, and for letting Stevens talk in circles until she made little sense. Cooper was especially interested in how Stevens appeared to support El-Sayed on Saturday night, then changed her mind by Sunday morning. There were reports that she spoke to Michigan Democratic Rep. Debbie Dingell around midnight Saturday and gave no indication she would switch her endorsement to Rogers. After the Stevens interview, Cooper interviewed Dingell. Talking about Cooper&rsquo;s conversation with Stevens, Dingell said, &ldquo;That was one of the most painful interviews I have ever watched.&rdquo; There is now talk that Stevens&rsquo; political career might be over after the strange weekend. But if that&rsquo;s true and she had any hopes of going into a career as a TV commentator, her awkward appearance with Cooper likely sabotaged that notion. Stevens says she is still a &ldquo;proud Democrat,&rdquo; but after flaming out on CNN, she turned to conservative Scott Jennings and his radio show on Monday. Independent journalist Aaron Rupar tweeted , &ldquo;It takes a lot to struggle through a softball Scott Jennings interview but Haley Stevens is pulling it off.&rdquo; For what it&rsquo;s worth, El-Sayed&rsquo;s campaign reported it raised $2.4 million on Sunday &mdash; the day when Stevens announced her endorsement for Rogers. So &hellip; not good? Henry Olsen, who writes the Margin of Victory newsletter for The Washington Post, weighed in with this midterm election analysis: &ldquo;A terrible night awaits the GOP, and even that might be an understatement.&rdquo; Olsen wrote, &ldquo;You don&rsquo;t have to be a prophet to see what&rsquo;s coming. No serious analyst thinks Republicans will have a good night as midterm returns roll in. The open question is whether it will be merely bad, terrible or something akin to being eaten by a Cyclops.&rdquo; Trump quote of the day President Donald Trump speaks at the Lincoln Memorial Reflecting Pool in Washington on Monday. (AP Photo/Alex Brandon) Speaking Monday in front of the Reflecting Pool in Washington, President Donald Trump actually said this : &ldquo;I've kept every single promise I made, except far more. I've actually kept far more promises than I made.&rdquo; The Bulwark&rsquo;s Sonny Bunch tweeted , &ldquo;Gonna need AI to solve the math on this one.&rdquo; Trump also hinted in his speech that today might not go well for Republicans, saying , &ldquo;For some reason, the sitting president doesn't win the midterms. We gave this a very strong shot.&rdquo; Trump did say that today is &ldquo;the most important midterm, I believe, in our lifetime.&rdquo; Adding up the ads You&rsquo;re probably sick of seeing political ads every time you turn on your TV. For many, the best part of Election Day is reaching the end of being overwhelmed by such ads. However, they are great business for local TV. The New York Times&rsquo; Katie Robertson wrote , &ldquo;The deluge of political advertising in this year&rsquo;s midterm elections is shattering previous records. Almost half of that money is going to a surprising place: your local TV channel.&rdquo; Robertson added, &ldquo;Advertisers, following the changing viewing habits of Americans, have increasingly shifted their marketing dollars to social platforms, streaming channels and other digital media. Yet when it comes to federal and state races, politicians and PACs are brushing aside concerns that TV ads are costlier to produce and less efficient in targeting prospective voters &mdash; and are showering local stations with billions of dollars.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s obviously good news for local TV stations and their parent companies. Robertson noted, &ldquo;The amount of money being spent on political advertising this election cycle is staggering, helping to boost the bottom lines of the biggest local broadcasting companies like Sinclair, Nexstar Media Group, Gray Media, Tegna and the E.W. Scripps Company.&rdquo; Wilbon to take leave ESPN&rsquo;s Michael Wilbon announced Monday that he is taking an indefinite leave of absence because of &ldquo;struggles with multiple health issues in recent months.&rdquo; In his social media statement, Wilbon, 67, wrote, &ldquo;This isn&rsquo;t retirement, but it is a trip to the injured list. Hopefully not an extended one.&rdquo; Wilbon has co-hosted the superb daily debate show &ldquo;Pardon the Interruption&rdquo; with Tony Kornheiser for the past 25 years. The two were formerly colleagues and columnists at The Washington Post. Wilbon will be stepping away from &ldquo;PTI&rdquo; as well as his duties on ESPN&rsquo;s NBA coverage. Wilbon wrote, &ldquo;Probably, I should have listened to medical professionals and people closest to me, yep Kornheiser included, who saw worrisome signs in recent years that suggested I take time away earlier. But I didn&rsquo;t. And as one close friend has said as my issues have become bigger, the bill has come due.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;The goal is to come out of it feeling much better than I do now and ready to return to sparring with Tony.&rdquo; Wilbon suffered a heart attack in 2008, and he has been diagnosed with Type 2 diabetes. Wilbon spoke more about his leave on Kornheiser&rsquo;s podcast on Monday. He noted that he has taken time off recently, including last week, adding, &ldquo;I was just exhausted; beyond exhausted, lifeless. And this has been going on for a while.&rdquo; In a statement , ESPN chairman Jimmy Pitaro said, &ldquo;Michael and his family have ESPN&rsquo;s full support as he takes time away to focus on his health and well-being. He&rsquo;s an important member of our ESPN team and widely respected by his peers, sports fans and so many others throughout our industry. We are here for Michael every step of the way and look forward to welcoming him back whenever he is ready.&rdquo; For now, &ldquo;Pardon the Interruption\" will turn to its regular rotation of fill-in co-hosts. That includes Frank Isola, Pablo Torre and Mina Kimes. Media tidbits The New York Times&rsquo; Sabrina Tavernise with &ldquo;Talking Politics With People Who &lsquo;Don&rsquo;t Talk Politics.&rsquo;&rdquo; Former Washington Post senior editor Milton Coleman has died. He was 79. The Post&rsquo;s Harrison Smith wrote that Coleman &ldquo;directed the paper&rsquo;s local coverage, mentored generations of younger journalists and championed diversity across the news industry, arguing that media outlets have a responsibility to credibly reflect the communities they serve.&rdquo; Check out Smith&rsquo;s story for more on the barrier-breaking editor. Salon&rsquo;s Amanda Marcotte with &ldquo;Fox News loses its grip on the culture wars.&rdquo; Speaking of Fox, CNN&rsquo;s Brian Stelter with &ldquo;Fox News ratings drop is due to &lsquo;viewer fatigue,&rsquo; Murdoch says on eve of midterms.&rdquo; One could argue that ratings are down for the conservative network because Republicans are in charge and things aren&rsquo;t going so well with the war in Iran and the economy, and dire midterm predictions for the GOP. Semafor&rsquo;s Ben Smith with &ldquo;How a Kansas City vlogger captivated New York society.&rdquo; Front Office Sports&rsquo; Dennis Young and Margaret Fleming with &ldquo;NFL Owners Shower Republicans With Millions in Midterms Cash.&rdquo; Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Matt Yoder with &ldquo;Why Joe Buck and Troy Aikman is the most painful breakup in sports media history.&rdquo; Hot type Ben Fritz, who covers the entertainment industry from The Wall Street Journal's Los Angeles bureau, with &ldquo;I Have Autism. Why the Elizabeth Holmes Documentary Was Uncomfortable to Watch.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Take the next step in your leadership career with Leadership Academy for Women , an advanced program for women in journalism with at least three years of leadership or management experience. Strengthen your leadership skills, expand your professional network and gain strategies for navigating challenges and advancing your career. Apply by Feb. 8, 2027. Strengthen your editing skills and earn an industry-recognized certificate from Poynter and ACES . Choose from Introductory, Intermediate or Advanced pathways covering current editing standards, tools and best practices. You can start at the level that&rsquo;s right for you and learn at your own pace. Build stronger fact-checking skills with Fact-Checking for Everyone , a new self-directed course from Poynter&rsquo;s International Fact-Checking Network. Learn how to evaluate sources, verify digital content and use professional fact-checking techniques in your everyday work. Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
