      "evidenceText": "Laurels and Darts - Columbia Journalism Review Laurels and Darts Political Journalists in Disarray Midterm missteps, a notable apology, and a sturgeon rescue. November 6, 2026 By Bill Grueskin ‘Readers Want Us to Stand for Something’ October 30, 2026 By Susie Banikarim The Plain Dealer decides to make an endorsement. Plus: Handing Trump a win, and cataloguing his family’s corruption. A Truth-Teller at Newsmax October 23, 2026 By Bill Grueskin “My role is to report on what’s unfolding here and explain what decisions made in Washington actually mean for the people living through this war,” Shelby Wilder says from Kyiv. Plus: Biases and absurdities. ABC’s Botched Cleanup October 16, 2026 By Susie Banikarim A lesson in how not to handle a mistake. Plus: Finding Roger Marshall, and change-making reporting on sexual assault in San Francisco. After Thirty Years, Fox Remains the House Ailes Built October 9, 2026 By Susie Banikarim Racism, misogyny, xenophobia, and consistently high ratings. Plus: ProPublica ’s school experiment, and a New Hampshire investigation with impact. Following the Political Money October 2, 2026 By S. Mitra Kalita Illuminating campaign finance reporting from LAist on the city’s mayoral race. Plus: A poor choice of words in the Cornell case, and the value of five-dollar obituaries. Solidarity, Finally September 25, 2026 By Susie Banikarim The press stands up for itself, together. Plus: A surrender to—not a settlement with—Paramount, and a great HBO crime doc. Following the Money, and Passing Along the Story September 18, 2026 By Susie Banikarim and Carolina Abbott Galvão A collaboration between KFF Health News and local outlets enabled reporters to produce a clearer picture of opioid settlement spending than the oversight body did. Plus: Cartoonish AI, and essential immigration crackdown data. The Newsroom That Conducted Its Own Cancer Study September 11, 2026 By Susie Banikarim Absent public records, NJ.com poured resources into research—and turned up devastating findings. Plus: Lost BET archives, and an extraordinary escape from Afghanistan, years after 9/11. Slippery Slop August 28, 2026 By Susie Banikarim The Wall Street Journal ’s opinion page embraces an AI submission. Plus: Tracking the expulsion of climate science from Florida textbooks, and putting out prison fires. Service to the Country August 21, 2026 By Susie Banikarim Stars and Stripes does its duty for the USS Abraham Lincoln —and the First Amendment. Plus: Poll psychosis and book tracking. Into the Flames August 14, 2026 By Susie Banikarim A brutal fire season in the Pacific Northwest. Plus: USA Today ’s Palantir partnership; separate and unequal in Tennessee. Chaos in Chattanooga August 7, 2026 By Susie Banikarim Newspaper owner calls the people abruptly laid off “simply a math problem.” Plus: Investigative reporting forces a Tulsa jail to close, and reexamining a false narrative in Seattle. Zohran Derangement Syndrome July 31, 2026 By Susie Banikarim NYC’s right-wing rags go full racist. Plus: Farmers pull a fast one; the human toll of deportation. Keeping Watch on ICE’s Local Impact July 24, 2026 By Susie Banikarim Steady coverage of Immigration and Customs Enforcement by outlets in Colorado and Connecticut. Plus: Axel Springer’s Peter Thiel party. Subpoenas on the Menu July 17, 2026 By Susie Banikarim The White House correspondents’ dinner is about to get weird(er). Plus, getting conned all over Texas. 1 2 3 &hellip; 6 The voice of journalism, since 1961 About Mission Masthead Privacy Policy Contact Support CJR Become a Member Donate Advertise Contact Us Copyright 2026, Columbia Journalism Review",
