      "evidenceText": "Coffeemaxxed lifestyle | The European Correspondent The European Correspondent Support the crowdfunding Projects About us Caffeine Coffeemaxxed lifestyle To quench my thirst for coffee, I scoured the internet for Europe's most café-loving cities. Sebastian Gräff | 26 Oct 2026 As a coffee addict, I dream of living in a city where cafés stay open for as much of the day as possible, ideally opening early and closing late. But my past geographical choices haven’t exactly served me well. To find out where I should have settled, I collected Google Maps listings for cafés in about 80 of Europe’s largest cities, covering roughly 15,000 active cafés. The data revealed a few interesting things beyond opening hours. London and Istanbul have the most café listings, but neither scores particularly well in customer reviews. If you’re after the best-rated cafés, Romania is a good choice: Bucharest’s cafés have a median rating of 4.8 out of 5, based on more than 30,000 reviews. Still, Bucharest wouldn’t satisfy me: its cafés stay open for about 10 hours a day. That’s much better than Leeds, where cafés are open for just seven hours on average. For a coffee fanatic like me, the Balkans and Istanbul may be the best bet: cafés in Tirana, Podgorica, Zagreb and Pristina stay open for around 15 hours a day. Groupings developed by cluster analysis of opening times and total amount of opening hours on an average working day (Wednesday) European cities can broadly be grouped by café opening hours, and these roughly follow cultural borders. You definitely won't find me moving to the UK or Ireland where cafés tend to open late in the morning and close early in the afternoon. My perfect match might just be the Ukrainian café culture where almost half of all cafés are open by 08.00 and stay open until at least 21.00. Sebastian Gräff Curious about Europe's biggest coffee cities? Some of the continent's best cafes (according to reviews) can be found in Vienna, Budapest and 