      "id": "fa3e1e47773e3231abd39de21e90c36770201c3d056182b2498fe6067339a0f2",
      "title": "Briefing: The AI image gotcha game",
      "url": "https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/microsoft-patents-ai-words-in-peoples-mouths",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T21:25:15.804Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://futurism.com/artificial-intelligence/microsoft-patents-ai-words-in-peoples-mouths",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Microsoft Patents an AI That Can Literally Put Words in People&#039;s Mouths in Real Time",
      "pageDescription": "Microsoft just filed a patent for an AI product that would be able to transform live video in real time to alter speakers&#039; words and faces.",
      "evidenceText": "Microsoft Patents an AI That Can Literally Put Words in People's Mouths in Real Time Shutterstock / Futurism Sign up to see the future, today Can’t-miss innovations from the bleeding edge of science and tech Email address Sign Up Thank you! Microsoft just filed a patent for an AI product that would be able to transform live video in real time to alter speakers’ words and faces. The patent, as first flagged by patent-tracker Patentlyze , is described by its inventors as a “near real-time artificial intelligence (AI) enhancement of video or augmented reality (AR) presentations of live events.” Using AI agents, the system would identify and alter or remove certain words, while editing a speaker’s face and mouth to match. The agents would rely on set parameters or user profiles to make the changes, and would also edit the video and audio just before and after a removed or replaced word to allow for “smooth transitions.” In an era of media and the internet increasingly defined by AI-fueled deepfakes, disinformation, and information-muddling slop, the system described in this patent may represent a new world of filtering tools that subtly alter reality as it takes place, in this case on a highly-personalized level. Viewers, the patent suggests, could consume whatever live event they want to without hearing what they don’t want to hear. The potential use cases floated in the patent vary. One possible application is real-time translation, with the system allowing for broadcasts and other live footage to be both heard and seen in a viewer’s preferred language. The patent also posits that such a tool could be used to remove filler words like “um” or “ah” from live speech, or to erase stutters or speech impediments. Other possible use cases, though, go much further. According to the patent, the system could be used to cut down on “personal words or phrases” like “coined terms,” as well as “colloquial terms” like the term “y’all” or the phrase “bite the bullet.” The patent also claims that the system could eliminate “mumbled words, mispronounced words, misused words, misspoken words, offensive words, insensitive words, or inappropriate words.” Replacement words, it adds, may come from an “internet search.” In other words, live broadcasts may not be so live anymore after all. Microsoft didn’t immediately respond to a request for comment. Dubbing has existed for ages, and video footage is edited all the time, both for normal and for insidious reasons. Automatically editing live videos in this way with the help of AI agents, however, could change how audiences fundamentally engage with live footage. As it stands, if people don’t like what they hear, they can simply turn something off; the system outlined by Microsoft describes an environment where people can instead adjust someone else’s speech to their personal taste. Language translation aside, does using a tool that eliminates errors, or erases words and phrases associated with a speaker’s local dialect, move us further into personalized bubbles? What’s so bad about hearing someone from the South say “y’all?” To that end, the patent does mention that an event’s original live video and audio may be recorded and stored for the sake of “ground truth,” nodding to potential misuse and confusion that could arise from such a tool. The kinds of live footage that could be transformed by the system include lectures, presentations, a range of television productions — awards ceremonies, game and cooking shows — and live sports, among several others. More on Big Tech patents: Meta Patents Technology to Detect Your Mood by Constantly Analyzing Your Tone of Voice .article-sidebar]:pt-0\"> Most Popular Artificial Intelligence It Appears That the AI Bubble Has Just Burst in China By Frank Landymore Ethics If Jensen Huang’s Wife Cuts Him Loose, She Could Get the Largest Divorce Settlement of All Time By Victor Tangermann Ethics Anthropic Just Committed a Shocking Crime Against the Environment By Victor Tangermann Ethics Nobel-Winning Computer Scientist Says AI Has Become Conscious: “We’re Toast” By Frank Landymore Astronomy Scientists Stumped by Why Mysterious Object Is Accelerating as a Japanese Spacecraft Rushes to Catch It By Victor Tangermann Read More More Artificial Intelligence Oxford Scientists Have an AI That Can Read Your Lips By Eleazer Corpuz Ethics “I Guess They Used AI or Something”: Trump Says BBC Journalists Deepfaked “Words in My Mouth, Literally” By Victor Tangermann Future Society Microsoft Wants to Censor Xbox Live Voice Chat in Real Time By Kristin Houser Ethics Sony Patents AI That Plays Video Games for You If You Get Stuck By Victor Tangermann Future Society Apple Secretly Working on AirPod Feature That Translates Speech in Real-Time By Frank Landymore Science & Energy Watch CRISPR Edit DNA in Real Time By Chelsea Gohd Meta Meta Patents Technology to Detect Your Mood by Constantly Analyzing Your Tone of Voice By Maggie Harrison Dupré Artificial Intelligence Scientists Suggest Sending AI to Aliens So They Can Talk to It in Real Time By Noor Al-Sibai SEE MORE",
      "structuredData": [
